General elections were held in [[Uruguay]] on 31 October 2004, alongside a [[2004 Uruguayan constitutional referendum|constitutional referendum]].<ref name=N1>[[Dieter Nohlen]] (2005) ''Elections in the Americas: A data handbook, Volume II'', p494 {{ISBN|978-0-19-928358-3}}</ref><ref name="corte2004">{{cite web |url=http://www.corteelectoral.gub.uy/gxportal/gxpfiles/elecciones/Elecciones%20Nacionales%202004.htm |title=2004 Uruguayan election results |publisher=Corte Electoral |accessdate=6 February 2014 |language=es |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160620113741/http://www.corteelectoral.gub.uy/gxportal/gxpfiles/elecciones/Elecciones%20Nacionales%202004.htm |archive-date=20 June 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The result was a victory for the [[Broad Front (Uruguay)|Broad Front]], marking the first time a party other than the [[Colorado Party (Uruguay)|Colorado Party]] or [[National Party (Uruguay)|National Party]] had held power since the two parties were formed in the 1830s.

Broad Front leader [[Tabaré Vázquez]] was elected president on his third attempt after his party won just over 50% of the vote, enough for him to win the presidency in a single round. To date, this is the only time that a presidential election has been decided without a runoff since the [[two-round system]] was introduced in 1999. The Broad Front also won majorities in the [[Chamber of Deputies of Uruguay|Chamber of Deputies]] and the [[Senate of Uruguay|Senate]].

==Presidential candidates==
The [[2004 Uruguayan presidential primaries|Uruguayan presidential primaries]] were held on 27 June 2004, to choose single candidates for every party.

